Create a self-contained Python 3 command-line script (intended to run under
Termux on Android, so use the Termux-style
`#!/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python` shebang) that recursively
scans the current working directory for `.lua` files and groups them into
folders named after a normalized base name. **Purpose:** Organize a loose
collection of Lua files by moving each into a folder derived from its name, so
that variants of the same script end up together. **Inputs:** - Command-line
arguments via `argparse`, with a program description of "Group recursively
collected Lua files into named folders." - `--apply` (boolean flag): by default
the script runs in dry-run mode and only prints the planned moves; with the
flag, it actually moves files. - The root to scan is the current working
directory (`Path.cwd()`); the script itself must be excluded from the scan by
comparing resolved paths. **Name normalization rules (applied in this order):**
1.
Take the file name and strip one or more trailing `.lua` extensions,
case-insensitively. 2.
Strip a trailing `_<digits>` suffix (e.g. `foo_2` → `foo`). 3.
Trim whitespace and lowercase the result. 4.
Replace runs of whitespace with `-`. 5.
Replace any run of characters that are not word characters, `.`, or `-` with
`-`. 6.
Collapse repeated `-` into one. 7.
Strip leading/trailing `-`, `.`, and `_`; if the result is empty, use
`ungrouped`. **Behavior:** - Use `pathlib.Path` throughout and the standard
library only (`argparse`, `re`, `shutil`, `collections.defaultdict`, `pathlib`).
Include `from __future__ import annotations` and type hints using
`list[Path]`-style annotations. - Recursively find all `*.lua` files under the
root (skip non-files and the script itself).
Group the files by normalized name using a `defaultdict(list)`. - For each
group, the target folder is a subdirectory of the root named after the
normalized key.
Each file's destination is `folder / original_filename`. - Skip files already
inside their correct target folder (no move needed). - If a destination already
exists, do not overwrite: generate a unique name by appending `_1`, `_2`, …
before the suffix, checking existence each time. - Create target folders as
needed (`mkdir` with parents and exist-ok semantics). - Dry-run (default): print
each planned move (source → destination) and a note that nothing was changed,
and tell the user to rerun with `--apply`.
With `--apply`, perform the moves with `shutil.move` and print each one. - Print
a summary line at the end with the number of files found and groups formed, in a
clear, plain-text format. **Error handling and edge cases:** - Handle filenames
that normalize to empty strings (fall back to `ungrouped`). - Handle name
collisions both within a group and against existing files. - Handle symlinks and
non-file entries by skipping them (`is_file()` check); compare resolved paths to
avoid moving the script itself. - If no `.lua` files are found, print a clear
message and exit without error. - Do not delete or overwrite any existing files.
**Execution:** Run as `python <script>` (or directly via the shebang) from the
directory to organize; first run without flags to review, then rerun with
`--apply` to commit the changes.
Use a `main()` function guarded by `if __name__ == "__main__":`. ---
